egree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill it solve? It removes manual burden and captures required events. Might be partial? Probably will solve main problem of meeting logging mandate. Score 4? Or 3 contributes? It seems would solve. I'll give 4. Evidence: automatic capture of mandated log elements ensures traceability and post-market monitoring compliance if properly deployed.\n\nOriginality: degree not only rare but ingenious...\n1 = common mundane boring\nMiddleware for logging is common. Score 1? Could be 2 interesting? Probably common. I'd say 1 = common mundane boring. Evidence: standardized logging middleware is a common engineering practice in regulated software development.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 paradigm breaking... changes relationship...\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship...\n2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving standard compliance\n\nThis is standard compliance logging practice, lab way unchanged? It removes manual burden but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1 paradigm preserving? It introduces tools but standard.\n\nDefinition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nMiddleware is new element. Might be slightly stretching. I'd choose 2.\n\nEvidence sentence: introduces internal APIs/libraries as new tooling while keeping compliance as a separate logging layer rather than integrating it into R&D.\n\nProvide output format. Need echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale