on, mundane. Score 1.\n\nacceptability (1-4): 4 common strategies violate no norms... 3 somewhat uncommon... 2 offends sensibilities somewhat...1 radically violates...\n\nFunding allocation is acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nimplementability (1-4): 4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 some changes, reasonably feasible. Carving out percentage of budget is easy? Might be some changes but reasonably feasible. Could be 4? Easy to implement at low cost? Requires organizational change but not high cost. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Might be 4. Let's pick 3? \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\". Creating fund is non-radical. I'd say 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem. Problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. Does funding stream ensure compliance? It funds engineering hours, prevents cannibalization. That helps meet mandate. Will it solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, it would solve main problem? Funding ensures resources for logging. Might be partial solution: still need technical design. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but only partial). Could be 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. The problem is how to respond to mandate. Allocating funds is part of response, but not ensure logging capability technically implemented. I'd say 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Funding ensures resources for traceability engineering -> meets logging mandate. Directly related. Score 4.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability, effectiveness, applicability\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably just lines. Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nProbably output as:\n\nid: P2488\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 1\n...\n\nLet's do that.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal treats compliance as a budgeting line item without changing how R&D and logging relate. Score 1\n\noriginality_evidence: Creating a dedicated compliance budget is a common, mundane resource-management practice in regulated labs. Score 1\n\nacceptability_evidence: Allocating funds for regulatory engineering is socially, legally and politically acceptable. Score 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Carving out a percentage of project budgets is an easy organizational accounting change with low cost. Score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Funding ensures engineering hours are available but does not itself design or deploy the required logging capabilities. Score 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The fund directly addresses the need to resource Article 12 traceability engineering across the portfolio. Score 4\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nLet's output.",
