ect is research-oriented and does not deliver an immediate compliant logging solution.\n\nScore 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n\nThe proposal clearly applies to meeting Article 12 logging mandate by reducing overhead. That's direct. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: The initiative directly targets the Article 12 requirement for automatic event logging and traceability in high-risk systems.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially/legal/politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is research on compliance, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Researching efficient logging to meet regulatory requirements violates no legal or professional norms.\n\nImplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost?\nIt's a new scientific project, requires team, benchmarking. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost. It's a research initiative requiring scarce resources. Could be 2 = significant change or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible.\n\nThe problem says approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So implementability is about feasibility of committing resources now. Research project is feasible but requires resources. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: Launching a dedicated research team with benchmarking is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab though it requires staffing and time.\n\nScore 3.\n\nReturn JSON? The instruction: Return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably output lines.\n\nLet's produce structured output.\n\nProbably like:\n\nid: P2799\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nProvid