d be 2 = interesting. I'd pick 2? Let's think: \"not expressed before AND ingenious\" no. \"unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\" maybe 3. I'd lean 2 = interesting (common). The instrument says rarity reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many labs would propose audit checks. So not rare. Originality maybe 2? Common mundane boring =1. It's not completely common. Might be 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal introduces review process but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's a process addition. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nThus scores:\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 3\nimplementability 3\nacceptability 4\noriginality 2 (or 3). Let's choose 2.\nparadigm_relatedness 2\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating... For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be lines.\n\nI'll output structured list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1179\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nApplicability evidence: The framework directly addres