ural persons. Proposal captures timestamps, database references, user IDs. Seems covers.\n\nScore 4? reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Probably 4 or 3. It helps but maybe not full lifetime monitoring? It says modular wrapper around existing telemetry tools that automatically captures timestamps... Might be enough. I'd say 4.\n\nBut maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, only partial. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal prevents researchers wasting man-hours building custom logs. That addresses implementation resource. I think effectiveness 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nUnified library is a tool/process. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new element (shared library). That's slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nShared internal logging library for compliance is fairly common. Probably common, mundane. Could be 1 or 2. It's a standard software engineering practice. I'd say common, mundane boring => 1.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is internal library, no violation. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change or expensive/difficult but not impossible\n\nAllocate two engineers to build modular wrapper around existing telemetry. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3. Not trivially easy? Could be easy at low cost. Two engineers is moderate. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nPossibly 4 = easy to implement at low cost. Two engineers maybe not low cost? Hmm. Probably 3.\n\nProvide output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON or lines.\n\nInstruction: Return rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer level)\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P2179\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's do that.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nMake sure sentences end with period.\n\nProvide final output.\n\n",
