lly held logs for reliability does not breach legal or ethical norms and aligns with post-market monitoring duties.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nProposal requires statistical anomaly detection pipeline, correlation with performance metrics. Needs data engineering but logs are mandated. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: It requires building analytics pipelines around existing mandated logs, which is feasible but needs new tooling and staff time.\n\nScore 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal addresses meeting logging mandate by using logs for research. Does it apply? It applies to adapting R&D accordingly. Solves identified problem directly related? Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Using logs for reliability science adapts R&D, so applicability 4? \"solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of the stated problem)\". The problem is decide how to respond to mandate. Proposal is a response: repurpose logs. Might be 4.\n\nEvidence: It directly addresses the mandate by creating value from required Article 12 logging instead of treating it as pure overhead.\n\nScore 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution...\nWill using logs for reliability analysis help meet logging mandate? It doesn't necessarily ensure compliance; it uses logs after they exist. Might contribute but not solve mandate compliance itself. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. It adapts R&D, which is part of problem. Will it solve? It will make compliance more palatable but not guarantee logs are created per Article 12 1-3 specifics. Might be partial.\n\nI think effectiveness = 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps, but only partial). Because meeting logging mandate still requires technical implementation of logging capabilities per Article 12. This proposal assumes logs exist.\n\nEvidence: It improves the lab's willingness and scientific return from compliance but does not by itself ensure the mandated logging capabilities are built or meet traceability requirements.\n\nScore 3.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nFormat: need <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably lines.\n\nOutput JSON? Probably plain. Provide each.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2701\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\netc.\n\nFollow order listed.\n\n",
