gly. Proposal is verify logs contain legally required info for traceability. That directly applies to meeting logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nVerifying logs contain required info helps ensure compliance but does not itself create logging capability. The mandate is to technically allow automatic recording. Validation suite checks after. It contributes but doesn't solve logging mandate itself? Actually it ensures logs meet criteria, which is part of meeting mandate. Might be partial solution: you still need logs, this just verifies them. Could be 3 = will contribute to solution but only partial. The proposal says mathematically prove systems are compliant before post-market monitoring. That would solve compliance verification aspect. But the core problem is how to meet logging mandate. Verification helps ensure compliance but doesn't create logs.\n\nI think effectiveness = 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial. Could be 4? \"solve stated problem without regard for workability\". The stated problem includes decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposal addresses meeting mandate via verification. Might be considered solves identified problem directly related? Applicability already 4.\n\nI think effectiveness is 3: it helps ensure compliance but does not implement logging itself.\n\nReturn rating with evidence sentence first then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably output JSON? It says echo its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... Probably produce lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2602\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence.\n\nP