o which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is a study to optimize UI/UX for verification identification. Does it solve logging mandate? Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons involved in verification. Study may help but doesn't implement logs. It investigates optimal patterns. That is partial solution at best. Effectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but only partial) =3. Could be 2? Unreasonable unlikely to solve problem. Actually study might improve compliance but not directly provide logs. Might be 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related =4\nSolves implied problem related =3\nMay have some benefit within special situation...=2\n...\nProposal targets Article 12(3)(d) identification requirement, which is part of logging mandate. So directly related. Might be 4. It applies directly to stated problem of meeting logging mandate for high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) Annex III. Yes.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis is a study on UI/UX for verification. Introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice? It integrates seamless secure identity verification into model's decision-review pipeline. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nStudy on UI/UX for human verification to satisfy identification requirements. Is that unusual? Probably interesting but not rare? Might be 2 = interesting. Could be 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. Intersection law and HCI is perhaps somewhat novel. I'd say 3.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating before number.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nOutput format? Probably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\"...\n\nProbably produce something like:\n\nid: P1031\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nLet's do concise.\n\nEnsure order listed: acceptability, implementability, effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The proposal involves user research to improve identity verification UI for legal compliance and does not violate laws or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Conducting user studies and integrating identity checks into review pipeline requires moderate staffing and design work but is feasible with existing R&D resources. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Optimizing verifier attribution UI can help meet Article 12(3)(d) logging requirements but does not itself build the automatic logging system mandated by Article 12. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The study direct