 preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nUnified logging infrastructure is new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but compliance remains logging practice. That matches 2. Could be 1? Standard compliance practice is logging library. Might be standard. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... ) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nStandardized logging library is common, mundane. So 1.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Probably just lines.\n\nReturn rating echoing its id exactly.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2836\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nMake sure order listed.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nImplementability evidence: Developing and mandating a middleware library across all high-risk pipelines requires engineering effort but uses existing software practices. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Mandating internal logging for compliance is a common, legally required practice that violates no norms. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Automated consistent logging reduces manual burden and supports traceability, though default fields may not fully cover Article 12(3) specifics for all systems. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The framework directly targets the Article 12 requirement to technically allow automatic event recording over the system lifetime. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a standardized tool and process for logging but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than redefining 