oblem. Solves identified problem directly related. Yes. Audit for traceability is directly related. Score 4.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness: 4 = paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance\n\nStress-test audits is standard compliance practice. Probably paradigm preserving? Internal audit simulating inspection is common. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (quarterly review). But still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nI think it's standard. Many labs do internal audits. Probably paradigm preserving =1.\n\nOriginality 1-4: not expressed before AND ingenious... 4, unusual interesting 3, interesting 2, common mundane boring 1.\n\nStress-test audits is common, mundane. Score 1.\n\nWorkability implementability: easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes =4\nsome changes reasonably feasible =3\nsignificant change expensive difficult but not impossible=2\ntotally infeasible=1\n\nQuarterly internal review requiring immediate retrieval of logs. Requires compliance team time, process. Some changes reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability: common strategies violate no norms =4\nsomewhat uncommon... do not offend =3\noffends sensibilities somewhat =2\nradically violates =1\n\nInternal audits are acceptable. Score 4.\n\nNow format output: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integ