 to match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. Capturing generic input data and user identifiers may not guarantee those specifics. Might be partial.\n\nEffectiveness definition: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). If implemented as described, does it solve? It automates event logging across diverse projects. Might be sufficient. I'd give 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, only partial. Because it may not guarantee recording of events relevant for risk situations etc.\n\nLet's choose 3.\n\nEvidence: Automated capture of timestamps and identifiers helps traceability but does not by itself guarantee logging of risk-relevant events or Annex III specifics like reference database.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\nDevelop internal API integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines. That's significant change across diverse projects. Might be reasonably feasible but requires engineering effort. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: Building and rolling out a shared library across existing pipelines requires engineering work and adoption coordination but is a standard software effort.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nAutomated logging for compliance is acceptable. No violation. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Automated compliance logging is a common, legally aligned practice that does not offend norms.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating echoing its id exactly. Provide evidence sentences then scores.\n\nLet's output structured.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1645\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence