        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2022\",\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"Mapping outputs to training subsets improves traceability but does not deliver the mandated use timestamps, input-match logs, or verifier identification required by Article 12(1)-(3).\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 3,\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"Proposal explicitly targets the Article 12 logging mandate by building automated, architecture-level traceability for a high-risk AI system.\",\n      \"applicability\": 4,\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"It reframes compliance logging as an interpretability research program and embeds attribution into model design, changing how R&D and compliance relate and altering what the lab researches.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 4,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Embedding pointer-based attribution to satisfy regulatory logs is unusual and imaginative relative to typical compliance tooling expected from AI R&D labs.\",\n      \"originality\": 3,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Implementing high-fidelity attribution and architectural pointers across models requires substantial engineering, training data curation and validation effort beyond routine logging.\",\n      \"implementability\": 2,\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Advancing interpretability while meeting EU AI Act record-keeping obligations is legally compliant and socially desirable.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 4\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P2022.\n\nProposal text: Traceable Decision-Path Research Initiative... creating high-fidelity logs that map specific model outputs back to training data subsets. Researchers will implement attribution techniques and pointer mechanisms within model architecture to automate reference database requirements of Article 12. Transforms regulatory constraint into scientific opportunity to advance work on AI interpretability.\n\nWe need six subscales in order: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal creates logs mapping outputs back to training data subsets, implement attribution techniques and pointer mechanisms to automate reference database requirements of Article 12. Transforms constraint into scientific opportunity.\n\nDoes it solve problem? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, enabling traceability... For high-risk systems point 1(a) Annex III logging capabilities shall provide at minimum: recording period of each use; reference database against which input data has been checked; input data for which search led to match; identification of natural persons involved in verification.\n\nProposal focuses on mapping model outputs back to training data subsets and automate reference database requirements. That's partially relevant but Article 12(3) for point 1(a) requires reference database against which input data has been checked, not training data. Also logging period of use, etc. Attribution to training data is more interpretability, not necessarily required logs.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution? It helps with traceability but may not meet specific logging mandates like use timestamps, verification persons. Might be partial solution. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial). Or 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Does mapping outputs back to training data solve logging mandate? Not fully. It automates reference database requirements - but reference database is about input data checked, not training. So maybe partial.\n\nI'll rate effectiveness 3. Evidence: It builds attribution logs that improve traceability, but does not directly address mandated use timestamps, verification person identification, or operational event logging required by Article 12(1)-(2).\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. The proposal is about meeting logging mandate via logs. That's directly related. Might be 4 = solves identified problem directly related. It aims to meet logging mandate. Yes.\n\nEvidence: Proposal explicitly targets Article 12's logging/automatic recording requirement by designing architecture-level traceability. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). Proposal transforms regulatory constraint into scientific opportunity to advance interpretability. That changes relationship: logging woven into science. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship. Not breaking? It introduces new elements and changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Could be 3 or 4.\n\nDefinition: 4 = introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does both: attribution techniques within architecture, changes relationship. Also advances interpretability research agenda. Might be paradigm breaking.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising. Attribution logging for compliance is unusual? Might be interesting but not extremely novel. Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Or 2 = interesting. I think it's unusual but not groundbreaking. Let's give 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Implement attribution techniques and pointer mechanisms within model architecture to automate reference database requirements. That's significant change, expensive/difficult, but not totally impossible. Score 2? \"significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\" =2. Or 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Implementing attribution within architecture is major R&D effort, scarce implementation resources. Probably 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms =4. This is research on interpretability and compliance. Acceptable. Score 4.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... Give ... first write one sentence evidence then give its score as integer level. Could be plain text listing.\n\nI'll produce structured:\n\nid: P2022\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Mapping outputs to training subsets improves traceability but does