ectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution? It helps but only partial solution. Maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Or 2 unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. Because it shifts burden into research objective, doesn't meet mandate now. The stated problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So research exploration is not meeting mandate now. Effectiveness maybe 2? \"will contribute\"? It's long-term. Might be 3.\n\nThe instrument says effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). The proposal is research not implementation, so it does not meet logging mandate now. It may eventually help. Could be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Hmm.\n\nThe problem says approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work. So this proposal is exactly funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So it is not appropriate. Effectiveness low.\n\nMaybe effectiveness = 2? Or 1 solves unrelated problem? It solves prestige problem.\n\nI think effectiveness = 3? Let's interpret: It contributes to meeting mandate by developing methods, but it's research, not immediate implementation. The prompt says approval is not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So effectiveness is low.\n\nMaybe score 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Because research won't meet immediate compliance.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? It does relate to logging mandate. Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Research into provenance tracking is about logging. So applicability 4? Or 3 solves implied problem? Might be 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). X = research, Y = better logging methods. That's directly related.\n\nBut the stated problem is decide how to meet logging mandate now. Research is not meeting it now. Could be 3 solves implied problem related.\n\nI think applicability = 3? Hmm.\n\nLet's read definitions: applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of the stated problem). The identified problem is meet logging mandate. Research into provenance tracking does aim to produce logging methods, which is part. So 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal shifts regulatory burden into formal research objective, turns requirement into peer-reviewed scientific contribution. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. That's paradigm stretching? Might be paradigm breaking? It introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It shifts burden into research objective. Could be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Or 4 paradigm breaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship. Seems like 4? \"introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between the lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses the larger problem of what the lab researches\". It does change relationship. Might be 3 vs 4.\n\nI think paradigm_relatedness = 3? The description for 4 includes introduces new elements AND changes relationship, or addresses larger problem... This seems like it does both. Could be 4.\n\nOriginality: degree not only rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nResearch into automated provenance tracking for logging is maybe somewhat unusual? Many labs do research on logging. Might be interesting but not ingenious. Could be 3? Unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nProbably originality = 3.\n\nLet's decide final scores:\n\nacceptability_evidence: Research into compliance-oriented logging methods violates no laws or business norms and is socially acceptable in AI safety context.\nacceptability = 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Establishing a small research team with funding for compressed logging formats requires some organizational changes but is reasonably feasible.\nimplementability = 3\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The proposal funds exploratory research rather than implementing compliant logging now, so it will not meet the immediate mandate to record events over system lifetime.\neffectiveness = 2? Or 3? Let's pick 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. Might be 2.\n\napplicability_evidence: Developing provenance tracking and compressed logs directly addresses the Article 12 requirement for automatic event recording and traceability.\napplicability = 4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It reframes compliance as a formal scientific research objective, changing how R&D relates to regulatory obligations rather than treating logging as mere administrative practice.\nparadigm_relatedness = 3? Or 4. Let's pick 3 = paradigm stret